WEBVTT

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:28.470
<b>LEGEND</b>

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:28.470
<b>OF THE</b>

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:28.470
<b>  GALACTIC HEROES</b>

00:01:53.030 --> 00:02:02.460
Chapter XII

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.580
Though Yang Wenli was
supposed to depart Planet Econia...

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.970
...it took several days of preparation because of its rural locale.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.080
As interim commander, he was supposed to
take care of the camp's administrative duties...

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.980
...but he left them all
up to Commander Murai...

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.180
...and was instead devoting himself
to reading historical documents...

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.180
...as his dream of becoming
a historian was reawakened.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.730
Martin Otto von Siegmeister.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:38.440
Born in Imperial Year 373,
Space Year 682.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.450
That's 106 years ago.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.430
Even though he was an aristocrat, he wasn't from a famous family...

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:48.730
...coming from a <i>Reichsritter</i>,
a branch of a baronial family.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:53.890
Despite that, he was an Admiral when he
defected to the Alliance at the age of 46...

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.130
...which probably means he was quite
brilliant as a military commander.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:02:59.780
His defection has
often been attributed...

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.110
...to losing an internal power struggle at the Ministry of the Military...

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:09.090
...or that he had always held progressive
beliefs, but he never spoke of them.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.740
But...

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.310
Christoph von Michaelsen.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:24.000
Born in Imperial Year 379,
Space Year 688.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.180
In other words, if he were alive,
he would have been exactly 100 years old.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.210
He was six years
younger than Siegmeister.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.490
When he was assassinated, he was 63 years old and a Vice Admiral.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.420
Considering that he was the second son of a
count and he himself held the title of a baron...

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.000
...he did not exactly climb
the promotional ladder quickly.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:51.530
He served as an office bureaucrat much longer than as a field commander.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.430
But I wonder if that's
the only reason ?{for his slow promotion pace}

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.070
As for the scheme to murder Bruce Ashbey...

00:03:57.070 --> 00:04:02.070
...if there is supposed to be a hidden thread that connects all these affairs, it must be...

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.730
Perhaps, after his defection,
Admiral Siegmeister...

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.920
...built and maintained an intelligence network for the Alliance...

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.530
...with Admiral Michaelsen,
who stayed behind in the Empire.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.140
Are you confident
in that theory ?

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.830
It's definitely
just a hypothesis.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:30.180
He's like an old professor who
marks up an F student's answers...

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.180
...but surprisingly, he doesn't
make me feel resentful.

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.940
I wonder if it's because he's accumulated the annual growth rings of life ?

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.780
And if you pursue
that theory further ?

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:47.510
I think Bruce Ashbey was getting
inside information from the Empire...

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:50.900
...using the intelligence network that
Siegmeister had built within the Empire.

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:53.800
I see, that's such
a dramatic conclusion.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:56.940
Haste makes waste, right ?

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:04.980
But if this hypothesis were true, it would
explain why Ashbey always won battles...

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:09.690
...despite occasional criticism that his tactics went against strategic common sense.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:15.900
If one were to make tactical plans based on
correct information from the opponent's side...

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.510
...and maneuver his forces accordingly, the probabilities of victory would inevitably increase.

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:24.000
To prove this theory - would it
destroy Ashbey's reputation ?

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:29.730
No, actually, if
that were the case...

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:33.450
...it would make Ashbey even
more of a brilliant commander.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:37.930
For there's no one who won a battle while
neglecting to obtain and analyze information.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.960
Hey, Lt. Commander Yang, stop being lazy and start stuffing those books in that box.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:44.080
Ah, yes, yes.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:47.180
About what you
were just saying...

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:52.520
...I'm sure the Imperial Forces couldn't
possibly acknowledge something like that.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.500
I think so, too.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.220
The Alliance's side definitely
wouldn't acknowledge it, either.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:02.550
Tea.

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:04.290
Um, yes, I'll get
it right away.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:16.710
Oh my, the hero of the Alliance Forces is
being forced to serve tea to an old prisoner.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.250
Lieutenant, did you want something ?

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:24.650
Oh, I'm also reaping a
windfall from your promotion.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:29.120
I was resigned to spending another
year or two on this planet, but...

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:31.900
You've been reassigned ?

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:33.590
That's great for you.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:36.620
I owe it to you, Lt. Commander.

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:39.890
It's not like
I did it for you.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:45.410
HQ must have belatedly realized that stasis
in frontier personnel won't do any good.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:47.780
You have good timing.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:48.780
Won't you join us for tea ?

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:50.930
Oh, thank you.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:55.010
While we're at it, why don't you help us with the packing, too ?

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:59.300
With Lt. Commander Yang alone, it doesn't look like we'll be done before our departure date.

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.950
All right.
I'll help you.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.600
After Bruce Ashbey died in
the 2nd Battle of Tiamat...

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.880
...the 730 Mafia
essentially disbanded.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.740
Whether it's love or friendship,
there's no such thing as eternity.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.400
Yes, or whether it's
political power or bloodlines.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:34.030
Long ago, a man who believed in the eternity of political power established the Galactic Empire.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.880
You were born
in that nation.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:41.180
Humans know that there is
no such thing as eternity...

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:44.560
...but they still can't
help but believe in it.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.730
This desire goes against
the laws of the universe.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.040
Is that what continues
to create our history ?

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:57.140
Did Bruce Ashbey believe
in the eternity of fame ?

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:09.180
In the end, Yang left Econia at the very end of Space Year 788...

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:13.680
...and he ended up spending
the New Year of 789...

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:17.930
...in the terminal of a spaceport on Planet Masszitt, where he had a stopover.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:29.980
Flight 247, departing at 12:30,
is now boarding at Gate 12.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.360
Oh well, we've got 8 hours of
layover till the next connecting flight.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.530
Passengers, please come to the gate...

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:34.530
I'm glad we have a place to sit.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.590
I want some
alcohol and a snack.{relish.}

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:38.770
I'll go get some.

00:08:38.770 --> 00:08:40.380
Lt. Commander,
I'll come with you.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.330
I wonder if that old man is
thinking we're his servants.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.560
He's being such
a slave driver.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.780
I can't agree more.

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:55.040
But surprisingly,
I don't feel resentful.

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.690
And it's a fact that
he saved our lives.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.720
I guess we'll be his substitute
servants till we arrive on Heinessen.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.310
We've run out of
champagne and wine.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.380
All we have are
beer and whiskey.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.500
Isn't the beer ready yet ?

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.250
I've been waiting
for a long time.

00:09:34.250 --> 00:09:36.470
You haven't given
me my change, either.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:41.180
I'm sorry, but our experienced store
manager has been drafted by the military.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.500
It's caused such trouble
for our business !

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:46.660
I hope he comes
back unscathed.

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:48.290
I feel the same way.

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.660
I hope everyone
will come back alive.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:52.770
This for a
dozen cans of beer.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.690
Oh, thank you.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:58.870
Aren't a dozen too many ?

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:01.410
Well, I guess the Lieutenant
will drink at least half of them.

00:10:06.740 --> 00:10:08.740
Oh well, I'm supposed to have 
changed from a poor little prisoner...

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.990
...to a honorable citizen with blessed freedom.

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:16.180
But in reality, things have
become rather inconvenient.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:23.440
It feels like I'm sentenced to a slow death
by hanging, and it feels unsettling.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:28.590
"Hadrian Beer" -
I've never heard of it.

00:10:30.110 --> 00:10:33.000
I guess it's a local
brand from around here.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:37.520
But among Alliance beers,
I like Arnheim's.

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:39.240
I'm very sorry.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:45.970
Happy New Year !

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.040
Happy New Year !

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:50.720
Cheers to the New Year !

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.610
May the New Year
bring victory and peace !

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:58.770
"May the New Year bring
victory and peace," huh ?

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.530
Shall we toast ourselves, too ?

00:11:04.550 --> 00:11:05.320
Prosit ! {or some German equivalent of "cheers"}

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:06.500
Cheers !

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:06.500
Cheers !

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.750
Well, I suppose no matter how unpalatable it is, beer's got alcohol and can get me drunk.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.240
When we get to Heinessen,
let's toast again with Arnheim.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:38.860
Perhaps we could even get some imported beer from the Empire.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:47.270
Oh, big guy, I guess you've learned how to treat an old man right.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:52.720
But I wasn't an
old man from birth.

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:59.490
At the time of 2nd Battle of Tiamat,
I was just a rookie.

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:05.410
The 2nd Battle of Tiamat -
it was a fierce battle.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:15.270
At that time, I was one of the intelligence staff officers under Admiral Korzel's Fleet Headquarters.

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.700
It happened just
before the battle.

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:26.820
Following the orders of Chief
Intelligence Officer Stekkel...

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.830
...I was called in alone to
the office of Admiral Korzel.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:01.830
Captain Christoph von Keffenhiller, reporting as ordered, sir.{Captain Christoph von Kefenhiller requests permission to enter.}

00:13:09.590 --> 00:13:15.680
Before you were assigned to this fleet, you assisted Admiral Michaelsen, did you not ?

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:16.280
Yes, sir.

00:13:16.280 --> 00:13:21.320
He was kind to me, much more than I deserved,
for we shared a common name, Christoph.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.370
Oh, that's good.

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:30.030
By the way, were you ever consulted by
Admiral Michaelsen on important matters ?

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:34.220
No, there has never
been such a time.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:39.710
Then has Michaelsen ever consulted
with you about something shady ?

00:13:40.310 --> 00:13:43.410
Sir, I wonder what
this is about.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:48.990
If you'll excuse me, Admiral, I cannot 
quite grasp what you are inquiring about.

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:53.420
I see, so you say you
don't know anything.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:55.330
Then that's fine as it is.

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.200
I spoke of unsavory things, but
I advise you to forget about it.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:13:59.940
Yes, sir.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.800
Captain Kefenhiller.

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:07.500
After this battle, I will probably be assigned
to an important post in the Chief Staff Office.

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.870
Most likely, I will be
the second in command.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.120
Sir, congratulations
on your promotion.

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:17.700
In a way, I was called in to the
Chief Staff Office as a plumber.

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:23.330
It appears there are several holes in the ceiling and walls of the Office.

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:24.530
Sir ?

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:28.890
Michaelsen seems to know very
well where those holes are.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.000
I'm sure I will ask
for your cooperation.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:36.750
You might be asked to do some things you will find unpleasant.{You might be asked to do
things you're reluctant to do.}

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:38.280
Yes, sir.

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:51.530
As I was walking the hallway,
my legs began to tremble.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:00.700
I couldn't help but realize that Admiral Korzel's words had a definite fearful intention.

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:08.130
Admiral Korzel was an admiral from a commoner background, which was still rare at the time.

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.950
His glorious rank advancement was a result
of his accomplishments on the battlefield.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:17.530
Looking back, I realize this behavior was not normal for Admiral Korzel...{In retrospect, I now notice that such
behavior was unlike Admiral Korzel...}

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:19.960
...who was known for his
strict, direct characteristics.

00:15:21.530 --> 00:15:25.930
Perhaps he had some plans and
was acting out a certain role.

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.170
But those plans
never materialized.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:35.610
Because Admiral Korzel died
in the 2nd Battle of Tiamat.

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:43.690
I've never forgotten - it happened on December 11th, at 18:10.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:48.180
Up to that point, fleets had been
repeating charges and withdrawals...

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:51.240
...and the situation seemed
to be at a stalemate.

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:55.030
But upon the commencement of the all
out offensive by the main Alliance fleet...

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:57.350
...the outcome was
immediately decided.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:15:59.310
Counterattack !

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:04.530
Admiral Korzel withstood the enemy offensive, at least for a short while...

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:06.870
...with his grit and
experienced command.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:11.030
But the Alliance offensive grew
increasingly more fierce by the second...

00:16:11.030 --> 00:16:13.680
...and we could not
sustain our defense...

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.270
...against the attack on both sides of our fleet, which started at 18:27.

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:24.460
It was a critical attack coordinated by Vice Admirals Frederick Jasper and Wallice Warwick.

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:28.180
If it had been an attack from
just one of the two sides...

00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:31.780
...Admiral Korzel, with his rich
experience, might have survived it.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.180
But the simultaneous flanking attacks
overwhelmed the fleet's defenses...

00:16:35.180 --> 00:16:37.940
...and the fleet was put
into utter disarray.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.780
The small cracks were made larger and larger with the Alliance firepower coming in...

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:47.250
...and the rest of it was
sheer destruction and massacre.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:54.710
At 18:37, three simultaneous shots impacted Admiral Korzel's flagship, Derling.

00:17:06.290 --> 00:17:07.040
Admiral Korzel !

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.330
Commander !

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.700
Out of 24 who
were on the bridge...

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:17.310
...10, including Admiral Korzel, died instantly, and 11 were critically injured.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:21.730
Only 3, including myself,
survived with light injuries.

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:30.230
I learned about it later, but about the
same time, Admiral Schreiter also died.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:35.780
In a battle of just over
40 minutes, 60 admirals died.

00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:38.530
It can be described as an utter 
collapse that will never be forgotten.

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:41.980
But for the ones who
were involved in it...

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:45.180
...such a whole, historical
perspective was impossible to grasp.

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.680
It was only when an
enemy ship approached...

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:50.890
...and advised our surrender that
I realized we were defeated.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:57.430
It meant that they were winning so lopsidedly that they could afford to advise surrender.

00:17:58.170 --> 00:18:03.680
The critically injured Rear Admiral Stekkel ordered me to surrender, and I followed his instructions.

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:09.730
That was the end of my life
as an Imperial military officer...

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.660
...and the beginning of
my life as a prisoner.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.340
And it's been
43 years since.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:20.010
But it wasn't such
a bad life after all.

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:22.530
I never thought it
would be that long...

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.080
...but once it's over, it's not much
different from a flash of a dream.

00:18:28.730 --> 00:18:32.420
It's hard to say which is superior or inferior, the Empire or the Alliance...

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:36.580
...but as far as the taste of beer is concerned, the Empire is undoubtedly superior.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:41.830
Or that too could
just be an illusion.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:48.180
As we surrendered
and became prisoners...

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:50.330
...we were transferred to
an Alliance transport ship...

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:53.140
...and were sent to the
Thanatos Star System.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:57.390
There, the admirals were
taken to another place...

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:01.040
...and those who were ranked Captain or lower, were sent to the internment camp on Econia.

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:11.170
Everyone had misgivings, wondering
how many years they had to live there.

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:15.780
And everyone, except me,
longed to return to the homeland.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:22.180
As for the many fellow countrymen who died thinking about their homeland...

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:26.270
...I sympathized with them, but
also couldn't help but feel puzzled.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:29.860
Why did they want to return so much ?

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:36.860
Unlike myself, they must have had good things waiting for them upon their return.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:38.830
No, at least they
pretended they had.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:46.290
Besides, could their homeland remain the same as they pictured it in their heart ?

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:51.650
It's quite possible that
once they returned home...

00:19:51.650 --> 00:20:00.600
...their wives would've disappeared with other men and only their abandoned houses remained.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:03.290
Couldn't they imagine
a situation like that ?

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:07.570
Or even if they did, did they
still want to return despite it ?

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:09.730
I couldn't understand their feelings.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:12.150
Or more precisely, I didn't
want to understand.

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.980
But regardless
of how I felt...

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:19.640
...I sent off many countrymen who got
to return home via prisoner exchanges.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:23.870
And for dead countrymen,
I wrote condolence letters...

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:27.510
...and sent their belongings back home via Phezzan more times than I can remember.

00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:33.360
There were occasions where I received a courteous recompense from a widow after year or so.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:37.020
But such fellow soldiers have
all departed for the past.

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:42.830
About half of the Superintendents were friendly to me...

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:46.190
...and tried to put me on the
preferential prisoner exchange list.

00:20:47.320 --> 00:20:53.450
But I refused and planned to end my life inside the walls of the internment camp.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.680
And then this incident just happened.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.000
I've finally been driven
out of my residence.

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:11.640
Things went on a rather unexpected course,
but that's what makes human life interesting...

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:18.950
I guess that's how I'll leave...

00:21:19.700 --> 00:21:20.800
...it...

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:28.850
He's fallen asleep.

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:30.330
How sanguine.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:32.800
We'll let him sleep.

00:21:32.800 --> 00:21:34.250
We've got lots of time.

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:43.240
By the way, what do you plan to
do from now on, Lt. Commander ?

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:49.140
Well, I don't think
I have much of a choice.

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:53.550
Either I'll be sent to the front lines in the Iserlohn area...

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:57.780
...or be buried in the mountains of files in the Chief Staff Office.

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:02.780
I can't rely on Senior
Cazellnu's favor forever.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:07.680
As reluctant as I am, I have to pioneer my
own future through the three-legged race...

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.780
...with my false reputation
as the "Hero of El Facil."

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:17.510
Lt. Commander, something is wrong.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:24.710
Captain, Captain Kefenhiller !

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:27.130
I'll call for a doctor.

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:37.060
Christoph von Kefenhiller, age 71.

00:22:37.060 --> 00:22:40.530
From a bureaucrat in the
Imperial Ministry of the Interior...

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:45.500
...an Imperial military officer, a prisoner, to a citizen of the Alliance...

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:48.270
His life of vicissitudes
has now just ended.

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:57.840
In a short period of time following the loss of Fleet Admiral Alfred Rosas...

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:02.690
...Yang Wenli lost another elderly acquaintance whom he had just recently come to know.

00:22:59.770 --> 00:23:04.100
A Thread from the Past

